  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nToday through mid next week (Wednesday)...\n\nThrough this weekend: Shortwave disturbance early this morning is\ndriving a complex of thunderstorms southward across the \nInterstate 20. This combined with stationary frontal zone, is\ndriving moist convergence and southward sinking complex of storms.\nThis still has some light west to northwesterly bulk shear (under\n20kts) that could drive some organization overnight, but\ninstability is waning and mostly elevated. Will have to continue\nto monitor potential to the south of the line and any areas that\ncan become rooted more in line with any outflow boundary\ncollision. Rainfall rates are heavy at times and backbuilding\nremains possible. Rain and storms will continue to be a concern in\nthe afternoon hours, but will become less focused as this\novernight convection may wipe out much in the way of redevelopment\nlater. Lastly, heat will not be as much of a concern, but areas in\nthe Highway 82 corridor could be seasonably warm in the low 90s,\nwith some heat indices exceeding 100F. However, confidence in\ndangerous heat remains on the low side, so holding off on anything\nin the HWO.\n\nAs the upper trough lifts northeast and frontal zone shifts out of\nthe area into the weekend, a cutoff upper low will form over the\nsoutheast to Atlantic seaboard and shift westward into the\nweekend. Rain and storm chances will continue to shift southeast\ninto the Pine Belt into late week and weekend, with the Interstate\n59 corridor the highest coverage (25 to 35 percent Friday to\nSaturday and 30 to 50 percent on Sunday). Mean ridge (595DM) will\nbuild over the Red River Valley area in the Southern Plains, with\nridging encroaching in our area from the west. This will help high\ntemps moderate back in the low to mid 90s (92F to 97F, with\nwarmest conditions in the northwest Delta region). Heat stress\nwill remain a low chance into the late weekend, with Sunday having\nsome low probabilities.\n\nNext week: Synoptic pattern will consist of mean ridge building\nout of the Red River Valley into the Mid MS River Valley, with an\nupper low sliding underneath the ridge into the Gulf Coast states.\nThis will drive some deeper moisture, moist convergence and rain\nand storm chances in southeast of the Natchez Trace through mid\nnext week (highest chances in the 25 to 45 percent range).\nHeat returns as highs moderate more into the mid 90s. With \nincreasingly moist thermal profiles and higher PBL moisture, \nmixing will be less a concern (dewpoints holding in the 72F to 78F\nrange). This will lead to potential dangerous heat from early \nthrough mid next week as heat indices peak near or in excess of \n105F. HWO graphics may be needed (potential the lowest a \"Limited\"\nrisk area) but timing is still several days away and could wait \nanother forecast cycle or two before introduction. /DC/",
